<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wainganga River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wainganga is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, about 580 km long, rising in the Seoni district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and flowing south through eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> to join the Wardha, forming the Pranhita, a tributary of the Godavari. Its fertile valley was the core of Gond kingdoms such as Chanda and later part of the Bhonsle kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wainganga-river.jpg" alt="Wainganga River" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sushant852 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Bridge_on_Wainganga_River_in_Bhandara_City-_2014-06-18_13-50.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Wainganga rises in the Mahadeo Hills near the village of Gopalganj in Seoni District, on the southern slopes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satpura-range">Satpura Range</a>, where the land stands at an average of 625 m above sea level. It winds south through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> across wide floodplains of sweeping meanders and low alluvial flats, between banks 10 to 15 m high. Its forested valley is thinly populated. The united stream it forms with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wardha-river">Wardha River</a> empties into the Godavari at Kaleshwaram in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telangana">Telangana</a>.</p><p>The river drains Balaghat District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhya-pradesh">Madhya Pradesh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandrapur">Chandrapur</a>, Gadchiroli, Bhandara, Gondia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagpur">Nagpur</a> districts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>. Its main tributaries are the Thel, Thanwar, Bagh, Chulband, Garhavi, Khobragadi and Kathani from the right, and the Hirri, Chandan, Bawanthari, Kanhan and Mul from the left. The longest is the Kanhan, at 275 km. The Thanwar valley lay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage route from South to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a>, and the village of Jhulpur on its bank was a halting place and temple town.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Balaghat, Bhandara, Seoni and Gadchiroli are the main cities on its banks, with Pauni, Tirora and Bramhapuri among the smaller towns, and the river is their chief source of water. By 2012 about 149 dams had been built in its basin, among them the Bheemgarh Dam in Seoni District, described as the biggest mud dam in Asia. The river carries goods and passengers under the National Waterways Project.</p><p>Rudyard Kipling set his Mowgli stories in The Jungle Book of 1894 and The Second Jungle Book of 1895 on the Wainganga, the water source of the jungle's people and the site of the Peace Rock. The forests of the Kanha National Park, which grew around the river, hold tigers, leopards and bears.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wainganga River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gond chiefs built a kingdom in the <b>Wainganga</b> and Wardha valleys from around the 13th century, traditionally beginning with Kol Bhil or Bhim Ballal Singh at Sirpur.</blockquote><a href="/state/chanda-kingdom">Chanda Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
